# LINE ENDINGS ARE LF EVERYWHERE, IN THE REPO AND IN THE WORKING TREE.
#
# This used to be `* text=auto`, which stores LF in the repository and checks
# out CRLF on Windows. That conversion is not free and it is not silent:
#
#   * EVERY `git add` REWRITES EVERY FILE IT TOUCHES. One commit of small edits
#     reported 66,043 insertions and 22,701 deletions across 12 files, because
#     the content git compared was a re-encoded copy rather than the file.
#
#   * IT BROKE A RUN. `git add -A` renormalised public/tier-models.js -- 4 MB
#     and 109,447 lines -- while `node scripts/mutate.mjs --all` was running.
#     esbuild read the file mid-rewrite and reported a syntax error at
#     4722:40 that does not exist. Three test files failed to build, and the
#     mutation run produced 14 consecutive INCONCLUSIVE results before
#     reporting "10 of 24 controls proved they exist" -- which reads as
#     fourteen weak controls and was nothing of the sort.
#
#   * IT MAKES TWO EDITORS DISAGREE. This repo is edited from Windows and from
#     a Linux mount of the same directory. One writes LF, the conversion turns
#     it into CRLF, the next add turns it back, and every command prints a wall
#     of "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings that nobody can read past.
#
# `eol=lf` keeps the working tree the same bytes as the repository, so there is
# nothing to convert and nothing to rewrite. Node, esbuild, wrangler and VS Code
# are all indifferent to line endings; git was the only thing that cared.
#
# ONE-TIME COST: after changing this, the working tree has to be renormalised
# once. See the note at the bottom.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must always use LF -- unchanged, and now the same as everything
# else rather than an exception.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Windows scripts are the one place CRLF is genuinely required.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binaries git must never treat as text. Getting this wrong corrupts them
# silently, which is worse than the churn this file exists to stop.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.hdr binary
*.zip binary
*.pdf binary

# TO APPLY, ONCE, WITH A CLEAN TREE (nothing uncommitted, nothing running):
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git status            # review -- repository content does not change
#     git commit -m "Normalise line endings to LF so git stops rewriting files"
#
# The repository already stores LF, so this rewrites the WORKING TREE only. If
# `git status` comes back empty afterwards, there was nothing to fix.
